WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.360
A parametrized curve describes position as a parameter changes

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.880
This formula traces a circle of radius two

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:18.000
Differentiating gives the velocity vector; its length is two, so equal changes in the parameter correspond to twice that much traveled arc length.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.010
Speed depends on parameterization, while the geometric path remains the same

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.292
Using arc length s as the parameter makes speed one

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:36.000
The unit tangent then records direction alone, separating how the curve turns from how quickly it is traversed.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.972
Curvature measures the rate at which the unit tangent changes per unit arc length

00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:48.238
A radius-two circle has curvature one half; a larger circle turns less sharply

00:00:48.238 --> 00:00:53.100
This is curve curvature, not the Gaussian curvature assigned to a surface.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.073
A surface uses two parameters instead of one

00:00:58.073 --> 00:01:03.583
On this paraboloid, changing u or v gives two independent tangent directions

00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:06.338
Their span is the tangent plane

00:01:06.338 --> 00:01:12.767
Independence of these parameter derivatives is the regularity condition preventing a collapsed coordinate patch.
